  "subtitle": "A world approaching Earth",
  "summary": "The Guardian Legend is Compile’s unusual combination of exploration and vertically scrolling shooting for the NES. Its heroine, the Guardian, must stop the alien world Naju before it reaches Earth. On the surface she moves in humanoid form through an interconnected labyrinth; inside its corridors she becomes a spacecraft. The objective links both forms: find and deactivate ten safety devices to trigger Naju’s destruction. Instead of placing two unrelated games beside each other, Compile makes exploration supply the routes and equipment for the next flight.",
  "body": [
    "The Guardian Legend is Compile’s unusual combination of exploration and vertically scrolling shooting for the NES. Its heroine, the Guardian, must stop the alien world Naju before it reaches Earth. On the surface she moves in humanoid form through an interconnected labyrinth; inside its corridors she becomes a spacecraft. The objective links both forms: find and deactivate ten safety devices to trigger Naju’s destruction. Instead of placing two unrelated games beside each other, Compile makes exploration supply the routes and equipment for the next flight.",
    "The Guardian carries a rapid-fire weapon with unlimited ammunition and a selection of more powerful secondary weapons. Those extra attacks consume power chips, which also serve as money in Naju’s shops. Firing freely can therefore spend a resource that might have bought an improvement later. New weapons and upgrades are found through exploration or awarded after bosses. The choice of what to use in a corridor belongs to a longer preparation, because the next area is connected to what the Guardian has already discovered.",
    "The surface gives the player room to decide where to investigate, while the shooting corridors turn that preparation into a concentrated test of movement and aim. A map that seems large and uncertain becomes a stream of immediate threats once the Guardian changes form. Returning to the labyrinth then restores the question of direction. This alternation is the game’s defining achievement: the pace can change sharply without abandoning the journey, since both halves concern the same hostile world and the equipment needed to survive it."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A world approaching Earth",
    "Chips for firepower and supplies",
    "Two views of the same journey"
  ],
